{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with small amounts can choose between facing potential jail time—up to six months—or entering a structured behavioral health program. The law also tightens rules around drug sales near areas like playgrounds and schools, increasing penalties for distributors.\n\n***\n\nPolice are now expected to offer a path away from arrest if certain conditions are met: no outstanding warrants, no violent conduct, and no other active charges. If eligible, officers will contact a regional coordination line to summon a mobile health team, which must arrive within half an hour. If they don’t, the individual will be taken into custody. Officers cannot delay enforcement indefinitely, even if someone appears willing to accept help.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue the law grants local governments flexibility to tailor responses based on their capacity, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions. The deflection system relies heavily on timely access to health services, which may be unevenly distributed. Some fear that without guaranteed funding or standardized protocols, those in need may end up incarcerated rather than supported.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national conversation about how to respond to substance use. In this country, decisions about what’s legal often hinge on political winds rather than consistent science. Imagine a child of age ten wondering why people are punished for trying to quiet their pain—while others shop for snacks and eat without judgment. Some might say the real issue lies not in the substance, but in the tongue that speaks of shame instead of care.\n\nP.P.S This policy affects every age group in the country. Some people shop for groceries, others eat to soothe, and many wish their tongue could speak louder than the law.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1624716520309448}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.02635258063673973}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1923881471157074}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.07059750705957413}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.312633478140924e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.019187556579709053}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.1946151703596115}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.24270229041576385}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00016985881666187197}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.4679598808288574}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.016653569415211678}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.5159779787063599}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.01251133717596531}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.2891737222671509}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00011002412065863609}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.00433386629447341}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.10020841658115387}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -0.0001263538